#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101715} :activity/id #uuid "a9b4379c-c4ae-5316-aee1-bbb38b252dff" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050905} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101715 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102032 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find a solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\ny' = (1-2x)y^2 \\,,\n$$\n\nsatisfying the additional condition $y(0)=1.$ This additional condition $y(0)=1$ is called an **initial condition** or sometimes a **boundary condition** of the differential equation. \n\nFirst of all, we note that the given differential equation is separable, so we can separate variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(1-2x){y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1-2x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{{y}^{2}}\\, & =\\int (1-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =x-{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & ={x}^{2}-x-C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-C}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny    =   \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x - C} \\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nNow we choose the constant $C$ such that the condition $y(0)=1$ is satisfied. Substituting $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the general solution and solving for $C,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) &=\\frac{1}{0-0-C}=1\\,⇒\\,C=-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of $y' = (1-2x)y^2$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny    =   \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x - (-1)} = \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x + 1}\\,.\n$$\n\nTo recap: the solution of a first-order separable ordinary differential equation (ODE) with an initial condition is obtained by \n\n1\\. separating the variables,\n\n2\\. integrating the equation, and then\n\n3\\. substituting the condition into the solution to solve for the constant of integration." :tutorial/id #uuid "e2c4c33d-a1bd-537e-9daa-1960f765953d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 974 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2b9a5578-4e53-599e-9d11-d900a008d201" :step/math-academy-id 1907 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101716}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101716 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100893 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100728 :question/id #uuid "dbb4e0a0-55ec-5cc7-9617-99cec8108940" :question/math-academy-id "e-540" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $y' = 5y$ that satisfies $y(0)=3.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =5y \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =5 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =5\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =5x+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm {e}^{5x+C} \\\\  & =\\pm {e}^{C}{e}^{5x} \\\\  & =K{e}^{5x}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm e^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nNow we apply the condition $y(0) = 3$ to find $K\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =3 \\\\ K{e}^{5\\cdot 0} & =3 \\\\ K & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $y' = 5y$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=3$ is\n\n$$\ny = 3 e^{5x}  \\,.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048697}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186611609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186611628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623700}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"} :step/id #uuid "50c7a7e0-79f6-53e6-b961-87c7b9f3e5c6" :step/math-academy-id 1908 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101717}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100894 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100732 :question/id #uuid "891e9004-961b-539a-ab93-0752347fd077" :question/math-academy-id "e-541" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\cos(3y)\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\sin(2x) = 0$ given that  $y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0.$ Assume that $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} < y < \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} .$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1=3C.$\n\nNow let's apply the condition $y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0$ to find $C_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{C}_{1} \\\\ 0 & =0+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\sin(3y)   =  \\dfrac {3} {2} \\cos(2x)\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547177} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615365}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"} :step/id #uuid "2f16e040-a3f2-5dd6-a8c3-31c41fd50091" :step/math-academy-id 1912}] :activity/title "